first.body:
{greeting}

I work with associations around {city} on roof condition assessments - the kind a board needs before a reserve study or a special assessment conversation.

{hook}

We'll walk the roofs, photograph what we find, and give you a written condition report with a realistic remaining-life estimate. No charge, and no obligation to use us for the work.

Would that be useful to your board this year?

followup.body:
{greeting}

I sent a note about a roof condition report for {company} and haven't heard back, which usually means one of two things - it's not a priority this year, or it went to the wrong person.

Which is it? If it's the second, point me at whoever handles maintenance and I'll take it from there.

breakup.body:
{greeting}

I'll stop emailing. If the board ever wants a straight answer on how much life is left in those roofs, my number's below and there's no charge for looking.

Good luck with the season.

first.body:
{greeting}

Most property managers I talk to don't need another roofer - they need one who picks up when a tenant reports a leak on a Friday afternoon.

{hook}

That's the part we're good at. Same-week response on active leaks, written condition reports on the rest so nothing surprises you at budget time.

Worth a short call to see if we'd be useful across your properties?

followup.body:
{greeting}

No reply on my last note, so let me make it easy - is roofing handled well enough right now that you don't need another option, or is it just not this month's problem?

Either answer is fine. I'd rather know than keep guessing.

breakup.body:
{greeting}

I'll leave you alone. If a roof ever goes sideways on one of your properties and you need someone out fast, my number's below.

No hard feelings either way.

first.body:
